            ? <><a href={b.url}>{b.name}</a><span aria-hidden="true"> › </span></>
            : <span aria-current="page">{b.name}</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Compare</p>
    <h1>Raven vs Jay: Size, Identification, and Differences</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens and jays are both corvids but vastly different in size and appearance. A Common Raven (700–1,600 g) is 5–8 times heavier than a Eurasian Jay or Blue Jay (140–190 g). Ravens are entirely glossy black; Eurasian Jays are pinkish with a blue wing patch; Blue Jays are blue and white. In practice, there is no field identification difficulty between ravens and jays — the size and colour differences are unmistakeable.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>At a Glance</h2>
    <table class="cmp-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Common Raven</th><th>Eurasian Jay</th><th>Blue Jay</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Weight</td><td>700–1,600 g</td><td>140–190 g</td><td>70–100 g</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Length</td><td>54–67 cm</td><td>32–37 cm</td><td>22–30 cm</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wingspan</td><td>115–150 cm</td><td>52–58 cm</td><td>34–43 cm</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Colour</td><td>All glossy black</td><td>Pinkish-brown, blue wing patch</td><td>Blue, white, black; crest</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bill</td><td>Large, heavy, curved</td><td>Medium, straight</td><td>Medium, straight</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tail</td><td>Wedge-shaped</td><td>Rounded</td><td>Long, rounded</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Call</td><td>Deep "kronk", "prruk"</td><td>Harsh screaming "skaaak"</td><td>"Jay-jay", screaming</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Flight style</td><td>Powerful, soaring; flap-glide</td><td>Undulating, flap-flap-glide</td><td>Undulating</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Primary habitat</td><td>Open country, mountains, forest edges</td><td>Broadleaf woodland</td><td>Mixed woodland, suburbs</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Size and Appearance</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The size difference between ravens and jays is dramatic. A Common Raven at 54–67 cm is approximately twice the length of a Eurasian Jay (32–37 cm) and roughly three times the length of a Blue Jay. In weight, ravens are 5–8× heavier. At any shared observation distance, the disparity is immediately obvious.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Colour is equally diagnostic. Ravens are entirely black with an iridescent sheen (blue-green-purple in good light). Eurasian Jays are unmistakeable: pinkish-brown body, white rump, black tail, and striking blue-and-black barred wing patch visible even in flight. Blue Jays are blue above with white underparts, a black necklace, and a prominent crest. No field identification challenge exists between ravens and jays.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Behaviour and Ecology</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens and jays are both highly intelligent corvids, but their ecological roles differ substantially:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Diet:</strong> ravens are generalist omnivores and significant scavengers — carrion is a major food source. Eurasian Jays specialise in acorn caching; they bury tens of thousands of acorns each autumn and are major agents of oak forest regeneration. Blue Jays similarly cache acorns and other nuts.</li>
      <li><strong>Social structure:</strong> ravens live in complex non-breeding flocks (floater population) and form long-term breeding pairs. Jays are less social — typically in pairs or small family groups, not forming large flocks.</li>
      <li><strong>Habitat:</strong> ravens prefer open landscapes (cliffs, tundra, mountains, desert) or forest edges; jays are woodland birds, particularly associated with oak and mixed deciduous forest.</li>
      <li><strong>Nest sites:</strong> ravens nest on cliff ledges or in tall tree crowns; jays build cup nests in the forks of trees, typically concealed in broadleaf canopy.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Shared Corvid Traits</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> As members of Corvidae, ravens and jays share a suite of family-level traits: high encephalisation quotient (large brain relative to body), excellent memory (both cache food and retrieve it accurately), complex vocalisations and mimicry ability, and demonstrated problem-solving in captive studies. Jays in some cognitive tasks (particularly those involving future planning and cache management) have been used as the foundational study species for corvid cognition; Western Scrub-Jays (now California Scrub-Jays) were the subjects of Clayton & Dickinson's pioneering episodic-like memory studies.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
